<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">French First Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1792 CE – 1804 CE · capital Paris</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The revolutionary republic that executed a king, fought most of Europe, passed through the Terror and the Directory, and ended in Napoleon's empire.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the storming of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuileries-palace">Tuileries</a> in August 1792, a newly elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> abolished the monarchy of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a> on 21 September 1792 and proclaimed the Republic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> was tried and executed in January 1793, while French armies fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Facing war and revolt, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">Convention</a> gave power to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/committee-of-public-safety">Committee of Public Safety</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilien-robespierre">Robespierre</a>; the Terror executed thousands before his fall in July 1794. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-directory">Directory</a> governed from 1795 amid corruption and coups. The Republic introduced the metric system, abolished feudal dues and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in 1794, and conquered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Belgium</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> seized power in the coup of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coup-of-18-brumaire">18 Brumaire</a> in 1799 and governed as First <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">Consul</a>, restoring order through the Concordat of 1801 and the Civil Code. In May 1804 the Republic was transformed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/french-empire">First French Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/french-first-republic.jpg" alt="French First Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1792 CE – 1804 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Paris</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Maximilien Robespierre, Paul Barras, Napoleon Bonaparte</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>France, Belgium</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>First Republic, National Convention France, French Directory</dd></dl></aside></div>
